). Not available.\n\n- In Bamboos: T3, T4, T6. If the player can get T5, then T3-4-5 and T4-5-6. But T5 is in the unshown tiles (T5:4). So if the player keeps T3 and T4, and draws T5, they can form two chows.\n\n- In Dots: B1, B2, B6. If they can get B3 and B4, maybe form chows. But not in hand.\n\nHonors: F1 and J2. Not part of chows.\n\nSo, the best chance for forming chows might be in the Bamboos if the player can get T5. Therefore, keeping T3 and T4 is important. So discarding T6 or T4 might be considered. If the player discards T6, they can keep T3 and T4, and if they draw T5, they can form T3-4-5 and T4-5-6. Alternatively, if they discard T4, they lose the possibility of forming the chow.\n\nTherefore, discarding T6 might be better than discarding T4. Let's check the valid actions: Play T6 is an option. If the player discards T6, they keep T3 and T4. If they draw T5, they can form two chows. That seems beneficial.\n\nAlternatively, if the player discards T4, they lose the chance to form the chow. So discarding T6 is better.\n\nLooking at the other options: Discarding W6, B6, W2, etc. Those might not contribute as much to forming chows. The Bamboos seem like the best bet for forming chows.\n\nAlso, the player has two pairs (B1 and F1). If they discard one of the single tiles (like T6), they can keep the pairs and work on forming chows in the Bamboos.\n\nAnother consideration is the unshown tiles. The unshown T5 is still available (4 copies), so the chance of drawing T5 is possible. Therefore, keeping T3 and T4 is strategic.\n\nSo the best move would be to discard T6. That way, the player keeps T3 and T4 in the Bamboos, increasing the chance to form chows if T5 is drawn. Also, T6 is a single tile, not part of any pair, so discarding it doesn't affect the existing pairs.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The player's hand has two pairs (B1 and F1) and potential for forming chows in the Bamboos suit if T5 is drawn. Discarding T6 preserves T3 and T4 for possible T3-4-5 and T4-5-6 chows.  \nReason: Discarding T6 maintains the potential for two chows in Bamboos if T5 is drawn, while preserving the existing pairs. T6 is a single tile not part of any pair or meld.  \nAnswer: Play T6"}
