But the unshown T2 has 4 left. So maybe possible. Similarly, W5 and W6: if I can get a W4, I could form W4-W5-W6. But W4 has 4 left. So that's another possible Chow.\n\nBut since I have to discard one tile, which one is least useful? Let's see:\n\n- W6: If I play W6, it's a single tile. But if someone has W5 or W7, they might take it for a Chow. But the unshown W5 and W7 have 4 and 3 left respectively. So maybe not the best to discard.\n\n- B2: There's only one B2. If I discard it, maybe someone can take it for a Chow if they have B1 or B3. The unshown B1 has 4 left, B3 has 4. So possible.\n\n- W3: I have one W3. But I already have a Chow of W1-W2-W3. If I discard W3, it might be useful for someone else to complete a Pung if they have two W3s. But since I have one, and others might have two, maybe not.\n\n- T4: If I discard T4, someone might take it for a Chow if they have T3 or T5. The unshown T3 has 3 left, T5 has 4. So possible.\n\n- J2: There's one J2. Honor tiles can be used for Pungs. If I discard J2, someone with two J2s could take it. But the unshown J2 has 3 left, so maybe not critical.\n\n- W5: Single tile. If I play W5, someone might need it for a Chow with W4 or W6. Unshown W4 has 4, W6 has 3. Possible.\n\n- T3: Single tile. If discarded, someone could take it for a Chow with T2 or T4. Unshown T2 has 4, T4 has 4. Possible.\n\n- B1: I have two B1s. If I discard one, someone might take it for a Pung if they have another B1. The unshown B1 has 4 left, so possible.\n\n- F1: I have two F1s. Discarding one could help others form a Pung if they have another F1. The unshown F1 has 4 left. So possible.\n\nNow, considering the melds of other players. The next player has melds of [T6, T7, T8] and [B4, B5, B6]. The player across has [T7, T8, T9]. The previous player has no melds. So looking at the other players' melds, they are using a lot of T7, T8, T9, B4-6, etc. So maybe they are building towards All Terminals or Full Flush in their suits.\n\nI need to avoid givi