ions.\nReason: B9 is a terminal tile (B9) which is less likely to be part of a chow sequence compared to W8/W9 or T3/T4. Discarding B9 maintains flexibility with the remaining tiles (W8, W9, T3, T4) for potential melds or pairs, while B9's scarcity in unshown tiles (3 remaining) reduces the risk of future dependency.\nAnswer: Play B9"}
