be someone else will discard a T5, allowing me to form a Chi. Alternatively, if I keep T4, I can try to get a T2 for a Chi of T2, T3, T4. \n\nBut the unshown T2 has 2 left. So both possibilities exist. \n\nAlternatively, considering the melds of other players, the next player has a Chi of T6, T7, T8. So if I have T3, T4, maybe they have T5. But not sure. \n\nAnother thought: My current hand has two Characters (W8, W9) and three Dots (T3, T4, T8). If I can form a Chi in Characters (W7, W8, W9) and a pair in Dots (T3/T8), that would be a valid hand. To form that Chi, I need W7. Since there are 3 W7s left, the chance of drawing or getting it is higher. \n\nSo if I discard T4, I can focus on getting a W7. If I discard T4, the remaining tiles are W8, W9, T3, T8. If I get a W7, that's a Chi. Then I have a pair of T3 or T8. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W9, I need a W7 and a W8. But I already have W8. \n\nSo playing T4 might be the better option here. Because it allows me to focus on getting a W7 for the Character Chi, which is possible given the remaining count. \n\nAlso, checking the valid actions, playing T4 is an option. So I think the best move is to Play T4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T4 allows forming a potential Chi in Characters (W7, W8, W9) if W7 is drawn, complementing existing melds and a pair in Dots.\nReason: Discarding T4 preserves W8/W9 for a possible Character Chi and leaves T3/T8 for a pair. With 3 W7s remaining, completing the Chi is viable. This move aligns with forming a concealed hand with high-point patterns like Pure Triple Chow or All Simples.\nAnswer: Play T4"}
