nd pair would be four melds and a pair. Wait, three pungs (each three tiles) plus a chow (three tiles) is four melds, and the pair is two tiles. That's 3*3 + 3 + 2 = 14 tiles. So yes, that works. So if I play B5, and then draw a T3, I can form a pair. But I don't have a T3 in my hand anymore if I play B5. Wait, no, if I play B5, my hand becomes W7, W8, W9, T3. Then if I draw a T3, I would have two T3s, making a pair. But the unshown T3 has 1 left. So if I play T3, there are 0 left. So if I play T3, then I can't draw it. But if I play B5, then T3 is still in the unshown with 1 left. So if I play B5 and then draw T3, I can form a pair. That seems possible.\n\nAlternatively, if I play W8, then my hand is W7, W9, T3, B5. Then I need to form a chow with W7 and W9. But that's not possible unless I have W8. So playing W8 would leave me with W7 and W9, which can't form a chow. So that's worse.\n\nIf I play W7, then my hand is W8, W9, T3, B5. Again, no chow. Similarly for W9.\n\nSo the best option is to play B5, keeping T3 in hand. Then, if I draw a T3, I can form a pair and complete the hand. Alternatively, if I play T3, then I can't form a pair with T3 anymore, so that's worse.\n\nAnother consideration is the scoring. My current melds include a pung of F1 (East Wind), which is my seat wind (East Wind is my seat, and the prevailing wind is South). Since I have a pung of East Wind, that gives me 2 points (Seat Wind). Also, having three pungs (the three in melds) gives me \"All Pungs\" for 6 points. But wait, All Pungs is worth 6 points. If I complete the hand with another pung or chow, but I'm not sure. Wait, the current melds are three pungs. Adding a chow and a pair would give me four melds and a pair. So the All Pungs pattern requires four pungs or kongs. But I have three pungs and a chow. So that doesn't count. So maybe my current hand isn't scoring as high as possible. But maybe there's another way.\n\nAlternatively, if I can form a concealed hand. If I play B5, then my